\section{Calibration against the measurement}

The reported reading is $\rho=0.437$ with interval $[0.393,0.480]$, and a stable advantage of
$+0.070$ over a \texttt{count}-style rival. Inverting the shape law, $1-2\alpha^3=0.437$ gives
$\alpha\approx0.6549$; we adopt the rational calibration $\alpha=33/50=0.66$.

\begin{theorem}[Predicted plateau lies in the reported interval]
$0.393\le 1-2\left(\tfrac{33}{50}\right)^{3}=0.425008\le0.480$.
\end{theorem}

\begin{theorem}[Calibrated plateau]\label{thm:cal}
Let $f$ be block-localised in a window of width $m\ge1$ inside $n\ge2$ ranks with
$50m\le33n$ (at most $66\%$ of the ranks starved). Then $\rho_n(f)\ge0.425$.
\end{theorem}

\begin{proof}
Theorem~\ref{thm:plateau} with $\alpha=33/50$ and $1-2(33/50)^3=0.425008\ge0.425$.
\end{proof}

\begin{theorem}[$T$ still beats \texttt{count}]\label{thm:beats}
Let $n\ge20$. Let $f_T$ be block-localised in a window of width $m_T\ge1$ with $50m_T\le33n$,
and let the rival be $R_{b,m_C}$ with $b+m_C\le n$ and $69n\le100m_C$. Then
\[
\rho_n(f_T)-\rho_n(R_{b,m_C})\ \ge\ 0.070 .
\]
\end{theorem}

\begin{proof}[Proof sketch]
Theorem~\ref{thm:cal} gives $\rho_n(f_T)\ge0.425$. For the rival, Theorem~\ref{thm:shape} with
$\alpha_C=m_C/n\ge0.69$ and $n^2-1\ge399$ gives
\[
\rho_n(R_{b,m_C})\le 1-2(0.69)^3+\tfrac{2}{399}=0.342982+0.005013=0.347995 .
\]
Subtracting, the gap is at least $0.425-0.348=0.077\ge0.070$.
\end{proof}

A three-percentage-point difference in starved width, amplified by the cube
($2(0.69^3-0.66^3)\approx0.0555$), accounts for the observed margin.

\section{Total starvation: the fragmentation floor}

\begin{definition}[Segment-wise scrambling]
Let $k,m\ge1$ and $n=km$. A map $f$ is \emph{segment-wise scrambled with $k$ segments of length
$m$} if for every $j<k$ it maps $[jm,jm+m)$ into itself and $i\mapsto f(jm+i)-jm$ is a rank
permutation of $\{0,\dots,m-1\}$.
\end{definition}

The dial has lost \emph{all} local resolution but still knows which segment each item lies in.

\begin{theorem}[Segment decoupling and damage bound]\label{thm:seg}
For segment-wise scrambled $f$,
\[
D_{km}(f)=\sum_{j=0}^{k-1} D_m\bigl(i\mapsto f(jm+i)-jm\bigr),
\qquad\text{hence}\qquad 3D_{km}(f)\le k\,(m^3-m).
\]
\end{theorem}

\begin{proof}[Proof sketch]
Split $\{0,\dots,km-1\}$ into segments and reindex; within segment $j$ the displacement
$(jm+i)-f(jm+i)$ equals $i-(f(jm+i)-jm)$. Then apply Theorem~\ref{thm:extremal} on each
segment.
\end{proof}

\begin{theorem}[Fragmentation floor]\label{thm:frag}
Let $f$ be segment-wise scrambled with $k\ge1$ segments of length $m$ and let $n=km\ge2$. Then
\[
\rho_n(f)\ \ge\ 1-\frac{2}{k^2},
\]
independently of the segment length $m$.
\end{theorem}

\begin{proof}[Proof sketch]
By Theorem~\ref{thm:seg}, $6D_n(f)\le 2k(m^3-m)$, so
\[
\rho_n(f)\ \ge\ 1-\frac{2k(m^3-m)}{(km)^3-km}
=1-\frac{2}{k^2}\cdot\frac{m^3-m}{m^3-m/k^2}\ \ge\ 1-\frac{2}{k^2},
\]
since $m^3-m\le m^3-m/k^2$ for $k\ge1$.
\end{proof}

\begin{corollary}
With $k\ge2$ segments, $\rho_n(f)\ge\tfrac12$, however long the segments.
\end{corollary}

Reversing every segment gives exactly $\rho=1-2k(m^3-m)/((km)^3-km)\to1-2/k^2$ as
$m\to\infty$, so the floor is approached from above and never attained at finite segment
length. For $k=2$, $m=3$ and $f=(2,1,0,5,4,3)$ one finds $D_6(f)=16$ and
$\rho_6(f)=1-96/210=0.542857$, above the floor $0.5$.

\begin{remark}
Squared displacement is a cubic resource: the whole range holds $B(n)\sim n^3/3$ units while
$k$ segments hold only $k B(n/k)\sim n^3/(3k^2)$. Fine distinctions are cheap, coarse order is
expensive; Spearman's coefficient is therefore overwhelmingly a measurement of coarse order,
and a starved dial destroys precisely the cheap component.
\end{remark}

\section{Algorithms}

\begin{enumerate}
\item \textbf{Exact Spearman evaluation.} Given a permutation of $\{0,\dots,n-1\}$, compute
$D=\sum(i-f(i))^2$ and return $1-6D/(n^3-n)$; $O(n)$ time, $O(1)$ space.
\item \textbf{Extremal displacement search.} For small $m$, enumerate all $m!$ permutations,
maximise $D$, and compare with $(m^3-m)/3$; $O(m!\,m)$ time, feasible to $m\approx9$. Yields
$0,0,2,8,20,40,70,112$ for $m=0,\dots,7$, confirming Theorem~\ref{thm:unique} numerically.
\item \textbf{Plateau curve and calibration.} Tabulate $1-2(m^3-m)/(n^3-n)$ against
$1-2\alpha^3$ on a grid; invert the shape law by $\alpha=((1-\rho)/2)^{1/3}$ to recover the
starved fraction from an observed correlation; $O(1)$ per point.
\item \textbf{Fragmentation sampler.} Build random segment-wise scramblings and verify
$\rho\ge1-2/k^2$; $O(km)$ per sample.
\end{enumerate}

\section{Discussion}

\paragraph{Scope.} The model claims: \emph{if} degradation takes the form of losing local
ordering resolution while preserving coarse order, \emph{then} the reading is a function of the
shape of the loss alone, bounded below by an explicit positive constant, and converges to it. A
dial that misplaces items across the whole range is not covered and can reach $\rho=-1$
(Theorem~\ref{thm:unique}). The empirical signature separating the two is exactly the plateau:
coarse damage produces size-dependent readings that keep falling; local starvation produces
size-independent readings at level $1-2\alpha^3$.

\paragraph{The two refuted hypotheses.} H1 fails by Theorem~\ref{thm:plateau} with
Corollary~\ref{cor:pos}; H3 fails by Theorem~\ref{thm:limit}. What survives of the monotonicity
intuition is Theorem~\ref{thm:mono}: degradation is strictly monotone in the window width, the
only monotone parameter the model admits.

\paragraph{Plateau height as a measurement.} The shape law inverts to
$\alpha=((1-\rho)/2)^{1/3}$. The observation $\rho=0.437$ gives $\alpha\approx0.655$; the
interval $[0.393,0.480]$ gives $\alpha\in[0.641,0.671]$. The cube root compresses uncertainty:
a $\pm0.044$ band on $\rho$ becomes $\pm0.015$ on $\alpha$.

\section{Future work}

\paragraph{Ties-aware plateau.} The measured target is starved: only $\approx0.89\%$ of items
are ``smooth'', so the target ranking is massively tied while the dial ranking is not. Spearman
with mid-ranks carries a different normalisation and the plateau constant should shift. Ties act
as a projection of the rank vector onto block-constant vectors, so the correlation splits into a
between-class part --- exactly the fragmentation quantity $1-2/k^2$ --- and a vanishing
within-class part. The decoupling identity of Theorem~\ref{thm:seg} already computes the
between-class part; only the mid-rank normalisation must be added.

\paragraph{Sharpness of the fragmentation floor.}
\begin{conjecture}
$1-2/k^2$ is the exact infimum of $\rho$ over all segment-wise scramblings with $k$ segments,
attained only in the limit of long segments.
\end{conjecture}
Segment displacements decouple completely, so the infimum is $k$ copies of a one-segment
extremal problem whose answer is Theorem~\ref{thm:unique}; what is missing is a limit statement
analogous to Theorem~\ref{thm:limit}.

\paragraph{Non-uniform fragmentation.} For a partition into blocks of sizes $m_1,\dots,m_k$
with $\sum m_j=n$, decoupling gives $\rho\ge1-2\sum_j (m_j/n)^3$ up to lower-order terms: the
floor is controlled by the third-power concentration of the partition. Identifying the
minimising partitions (intuitively the most unequal) is the natural next theorem.

\paragraph{Mixed damage.} Superimposing a loss of coarse order on local scrambling would
quantify how much coarse damage breaks the plateau; since coarse damage is expensive in budget
terms, one expects a sharp threshold.

\paragraph{Beyond Spearman.} Kendall's $\tau$ counts inversions, a quadratic resource
($\binom{n}{2}$), so the analogous laws should read $\tau\ge1-2\alpha^2$ and $\tau\ge1-1/k$.
Proving these would confirm that the exponent of the plateau law is set by the degree of the
statistic's normalising budget.

\section{Conclusion}

A rank-correlation reading that stops falling is the signature of a specific kind of
information loss. When a ranking heuristic loses only \emph{local} ordering resolution --- inside
a window of relative width $\alpha$, or inside each of $k$ segments --- its Spearman correlation
obeys a sharp, size-free law: $\rho\ge1-2\alpha^3$ in the first case, $\rho\ge1-2/k^2$ in the
second, both attained in the limit. Calibrating the first law to a starved fraction of $0.66$
yields the plateau value $0.425008$, inside the observed interval $[0.393,0.480]$, and a
guaranteed margin of $0.070$ over a rival starved on $0.69$ of its range. The mathematical core
is a single exact identity: a ranking and its mirror always split the displacement budget
$n^3-n$ between them.

